On 2008-03-12, sjdevnull@yahoo.com <sjdevnull@yahoo.com> wrote:
> On Mar 12, 8:12 am, Derek Ray <de...@moot.its.only.a.spamtrap.org>
>> On 2008-03-12, sjdevnull@yahoo.com <sjdevnull@yahoo.com> wrote:
>> > I don't personally like the overall
>> > design goals (removal of binary resistances, adding more beankeeping)
>> Which parts are you thinking of as "beankeeping"?
>
> The restore ability changes are one example.  They're nice early on
> when resources are scarce to have to deal with RA, but later on it's
> just one more thing to stuff in the sack and carry around with you (or
> another spellbook to track down).  Basically anything that increases
> the amount of one-purpose-only equipment you need to tote around is
> annoying (to me, anyway).

...Are you really losing that many ability points late in the game?
If so, could you explain how?  Is it something that could be avoided by
not making "stat abuse" decisions?

The removal of numeric-stat restoration from the unihorn was necessary to 
make all forms of "restore ability" even worthwhile in the first place;
unihorns were so easily obtainable so early in the game that the only
stat loss that was meaningful for any length of time was Str -- and only
that from early poisoned pits or poisoned arrows.  Even _that_ was easily
circumvented by prayer and knowing where to put your thumb on the scale,
so to speak.

Then, of course, I lowered the RA spellbook to level 2 so that
almost all characters who stumbled across it could use it effectively,
and the potions themselves were always common enough, just meaningless
in comparison.

> Magical eyes are sort of "player-beancounting"; you just blind
> yourself and beat them senseless, so after the first couple of times
> it's mostly an annoying "okay, now do a) b) c)" hoop to jump through--
> but if there's other stuff around then blinding yourself is actually
> meaningful.  If they only appeared in zoos and summon nasties (and
> other places where they're contributing toward a larger problem) they
> wouldn't annoy me.

They've been an explicit target in nasty() since they were introduced;
the original point of the design was to create a monster that wasn't all
_that_ bad on its own, but was a royal nuisance when you were having to
deal with a bunch of other critters.  It sounds like that goal
succeeded admirably.

You shouldn't be seeing _that_ many of them, honestly; if there are too
many popping up, I can increase the rarity slightly (as they _are_ only
effective in concert with something large and unpleasant.)

> Grey fungus: Yet another "go find some rocks and chuck them at it"
> thing.  Having a few is interesting, and provides a challenge to new
> players.  At least some of them provide some intrinsics.  But I'm not
> sure we needed another.

This particular one is not infravisible, which is the point.  (The fungus 
does provide intrinsics if you eat it; I suspect nobody has gone 
source-diving for that one yet, so it's not surprising you didn't know.)

> MC changes: basically forcing the player to do some run away and
> missile fire stuff more often.  Not something that's difficult to do
> 90% of the time, just vaguely annoying.  

... OK?  Personally, given that missile fire is sadly unused in Nethack
currently (and can be completely ignored by most roles), adding some 
encouragement for players to actually use missile weaponry is certainly
an improvement in my book.

> Having to go through this well-known pattern (back off, throw missiles, 
> back off, throw missiles) that just takes a little longer than 
> whack-whack-whack is't more fun or skillful.  

No, it _is_ skillful; it's just a level of skill that you've just already 
passed in your Nethack career.  Fun?  Well, I've already noticed one
thing; there is a class of player for whom Nethack will never be fun
again, because they're simply too good at it.  Those players have all 
gone to Crawl, and rightly so.  I don't think Spork should try to address 
those players' needs -- perhaps you are one of those players?

> Again, if you get swamped then this change
> suddenly becomes an actual challenge, so I don't really know what to
> do about that.

I'd rather not be forced to mob the player _every_ time I want to make
him stop and think, even though it seems sometimes like that's what it
takes to get a well-kitted-out character's attention.

> It's essentially tied to the problem of complete resistances: having
> those lets you introduce a challenge, 

suspect you meant "incomplete" here?

> but once the player's dealt with it for a while and it's become 
> monotonous, they can get protection from it and move on to other 
> challenges.  The problem with Vanilla is that there aren't other 
> challenges between mid-game and Rodney.  

I would say there are not enough challenges between "post mines/sokoban"
and Rodney, personally.  The Castle in vanilla is no challenge at all;
the Castle in Spork is somewhat more so, and the new bigroom and medusa
levels are good for some surprise.

This goes back to what I said originally though; the midgame still needs
some challenges to be added so that it's not just a slog to Rodney.
Unfortunately, sometimes the way to add challenge is to remove things;
hence the resistances.  When the only thing that can affect the player
is physical or spell damage, that gets boring quickly.

